    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-creation limit following the codebase's existing `CityOwnerLimitException` pattern exactly.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`service/DailyOwnerLimitException.java`** (new) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` mirroring `CityOwnerLimitException`, constructed with the registration date and limit.\n\n2. **`service/ClinicServiceImpl.java`** \u2014 in `saveOwner`, added an `assertDailyCapacity(owner)` call inside the `owner.isNew()` block (right before the city-capacity check, after `registrationDate` is defaulted to today). New helpers:\n   - `DAILY_OWNER_LIMIT = 100L`\n   - `assertDailyCapacity` \u2014 throws when the owner's `registrationDate` already has \u2265100 owners.\n   - `countOwnersRegisteredOn(date)` \u2014 counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals that date (same `findAll().stream()` idiom as `countOwnersInCity`).\n\n3. **`rest/advice/ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` for `DailyOwnerLimitException` mapping it to `HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` (429), building the same RFC-7807 `ProblemDetail` response as the other handlers.\n\n**Design notes:** The check counts by the new owner's `registrationDate`, which defaults to `LocalDate.now()` \u2014 so \"created today\" is enforced per registration day, and an explicitly-supplied date is counted against its own day. The check runs only for new owners, so updates aren't affected. The acceptance test (`createMany(100)` \u2192 101st rejected) passes, and its log confirms the 429 handler firing.",
